{#
  The tenant CUDN with EVPN as its transport.

  Two things change relative to the phase-3 CUDN, and both live INSIDE
  spec.network - not at the top of spec:

    network.transport: EVPN   - east/west pod traffic for this network stops
                                using Geneve and uses VXLAN instead, with the
                                EVPN control plane distributing reachability.
                                The UDN's addressing, isolation and role are
                                unchanged. The field is immutable, which is
                                why the apply tasks recreate the CUDN rather
                                than patching it when moving between phases.

    network.evpn              - which VTEP to use, and the VNI/route-target
                                per VRF type.

  Layer3 topology takes ipVRF and the API *forbids* macVRF here; Layer2 takes
  macVRF (required) and may add ipVRF. Layer3 is the right shape for routed
  tenant segmentation, which is what this lab's tenants are, and it is the
  direct continuation of the VRF-Lite phase - the same tenant, the same
  subnet, carried over EVPN instead of a VLAN handoff. See the README for
  what a Layer2/macVRF tenant would additionally need on the fabric side.

  routeTarget is set explicitly rather than left to default to <ASN>:<VNI>,
  because the fabric side has to match and an explicit value on both ends is
  one less thing to derive when a route is not appearing where it should. Two
  UDNs sharing a route target of the same VRF type would leak routes between
  tenants - exactly what this lab is trying to prove does not happen - so the
  values are distinct per tenant. The API requires the form
  <global>:<local> where global is an ASN or an IPv4 address; {{ clab_spine_asn }}
  is a 2-byte ASN so the local part may be up to 4294967295.
#}
apiVersion: k8s.ovn.org/v1
kind: ClusterUserDefinedNetwork
metadata:
  name: {{ tenant.name }}
  labels:
    {{ udn_bgp_cudn_label_key }}: "enabled"
    udn-lab-phase: evpn
spec:
  namespaceSelector:
    matchLabels:
      udn-tenant: {{ tenant.name }}
{% if tenant.topology | default('Layer3') == 'Layer2' %}
  network:
    topology: Layer2
    layer2:
      role: Primary
      subnets:
        - {{ tenant.udn_subnet }}
{#
   What this cluster must NOT allocate from, which is two separate things
   that happen to be expressed the same way.

   1. The fabric side. This tenant's EVPN transport is a macVRF, so its
      external endpoint sits INSIDE this subnet, on leaf2's side of the VXLAN
      tunnel - {{ tenant.evpn_l2_external_host }}. OVN knows nothing about it
      and would happily allocate the same address to a pod, putting two
      machines on one address in one broadcast domain. That is a
      duplicate-address bug, not the deliberate cross-network overlap this lab
      is about.

   2. The other clusters' half of the prefix. A stretched Layer2 network is
      one broadcast domain spanning every cluster on the L2VNI, but each
      cluster's OVN-Kubernetes allocates from the subnet knowing nothing about
      the others - so without a split they both hand out the low addresses and
      two pods in different clusters end up on one address. There is no
      cross-cluster IPAM to lean on; carving the prefix per cluster is how it
      is done.

   evpn_l2_excludes[<cluster>] carries the answer for both, per cluster.
   evpn_l2_exclude is the single-cluster form and is the fallback when this
   cluster has no entry - which is correct for a fabric with one cluster on
   it, where only reason 1 applies.
#}
{% set l2_excludes = (tenant.evpn_l2_excludes | default({})).get(udn_bgp_cluster)
                     or ([tenant.evpn_l2_exclude] if tenant.evpn_l2_exclude is defined else []) %}
{% if l2_excludes %}
      # Reserved: see the note above. Cluster '{{ udn_bgp_cluster }}' allocates
      # from {{ tenant.udn_subnet }} minus these.
      excludeSubnets:
{% for x in l2_excludes %}
        - {{ x }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
      ipam:
        lifecycle: Persistent
    transport: EVPN
    evpn:
      vtep: evpn-vtep
      # macVRF, not ipVRF: a Layer2 network is a broadcast domain, so what
      # EVPN carries for it is MAC reachability - type-2 routes on an L2VNI -
      # rather than the type-5 prefix routes a Layer3 network needs. The API
      # enforces the split: macVRF is required for Layer2 and rejected for
      # Layer3, and ipVRF the other way round.
      #
      # This is the combination that stretches the broadcast domain across
      # the fabric rather than just across the cluster's own overlay, which
      # is what makes a live migration invisible to anything outside.
      macVRF:
        vni: {{ tenant.evpn_mac_vni }}
        routeTarget: "{{ clab_spine_asn }}:{{ tenant.evpn_mac_vni }}"
{% else %}
  network:
    topology: Layer3
    layer3:
      role: Primary
      subnets:
        - cidr: {{ tenant.udn_subnet }}
          hostSubnet: {{ tenant.udn_host_subnet }}
    transport: EVPN
    evpn:
      vtep: evpn-vtep
      ipVRF:
        vni: {{ tenant.evpn_ip_vni }}
        routeTarget: "{{ clab_spine_asn }}:{{ tenant.evpn_ip_vni }}"
{% endif %}
